From 2ba0aff9f3d3722df061dd939c97e34e278314b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: webOS Phoenix <dev@webosphoenix.org>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 16:19:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Network position without radios or a third-party key, and the
 user's own

webOS Phoenix runs on VMs, tablets on a wired network and boards without
GNSS, where the location service never answered: the network handler only
asks a server when it has Wi-Fi access points or cells to send, and a
hybrid request whose GPS source cannot start failed before the network
handler was asked.

- network: a third server, the platform's own Ichnaea-compatible
  geolocate service, named in /etc/location/geolocate.conf
  ([geolocate] url=...). It is the default when that file names one
  ("phoenix"); beaconDB stays the default otherwise.
- network: with no Wi-Fi or cell data (none within 4 s of a request, or on
  the 60 s update with neither radio on), ask for a position by IP address
  ({"considerIp":true}, which beaconDB, Google and Phoenix's service all
  answer), again at most every 10 minutes once there is a fix.
- network: a "manual" provider: the position the user set
  (setNetworkProvider {provider: "manual", position: {latitude,
  longitude, [accuracy], [name]}}), kept in luna-prefs (manualPosition),
  answered at once and without a connection. getNetworkProvider reports
  it, and "phoenix" when configured, in providers.
- getLocationUpdates: in a hybrid request, a GPS handler whose source
  cannot start (no receiver; the nyx module cannot open its port) leaves
  the request to the network handler instead of failing it.
- geocoding: a URL that is not Google's is taken as it is, unsigned and
  without the key (webOS Phoenix's /v1/location/geocode/json).

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS Phoenix configuration]
